# Check contract for the clang-tidy runs in .githooks/pre-commit (safe --fix
# subset) and .githooks/pre-push (full sweep, hard block).
#
# Owned by this repository, like its sibling .clang-format.
#
# Where this file departs from stock Google style the reason is recorded
# inline, with the measurement that justifies it. Nothing is turned off to
# avoid work.
---
# THE RATCHET IS EMPTY, and issue #121 -- which tracked emptying it -- is
# closed. This file used to carry a second list below the exemptions: checks
# disabled by name with their unfixed finding counts, so that the rest of
# bugprone-*/modernize-*/performance-*/readability-* could block at push before
# the tree satisfied all of it. It started at 26 checks and ran to zero. Every
# check in those four groups is now enabled except the four structural
# exemptions below, and each of those is off because the CHECK is wrong for
# this codebase -- never because its findings are unfixed.
#
# So there is no list here to add a name to any more. A clang-tidy warning at
# push is new code's doing: fix it. Putting a name below has to meet the same
# standard the four already there meet -- a structural cause, and a measurement
# -- and it is a case to argue, not a way to get a push through.
#
# Six checks are disabled below the stock set, each for a structural reason
# rather than to dodge work. Two of them are the long-standing pair at the top
# of the scalar, whose reasons were never written down until issue #121 closed:
#
#   modernize-use-trailing-return-type -- 731 hits, i.e. every function in the
#     tree. It asks for `auto f() -> T` everywhere. Google style does not use
#     that spelling and neither does this codebase; the check is a whole-tree
#     style preference, not a defect class.
#
#   readability-magic-numbers -- 2058 hits, the largest count this config has
#     ever measured. 1382 are in tests/, where the literal IS the assertion
#     (`REQUIRE(v == 3.0)`), and 495 in benchmarks/, where they are instance
#     data and published tolerances. Of the ~180 in src/ and include/cbls/, the
#     ones worth naming already are named -- kEps, kMpsInf, kStrideGrowth and
#     the rest of the k-constants the naming options below encode. Turning this
#     on would demand a named constant for every coefficient in a formula
#     transcribed from a paper, which is the same argument as
#     readability-identifier-length below.
#
# The other four:
#
#   bugprone-chained-comparison -- Catch2's REQUIRE/CHECK decompose into an
#     expression template, so every `REQUIRE(a == b)` reads to this check as the
#     chained comparison `v0 <= v1 > v2`. Measured: 548 hits, all in tests/, all
#     of them the macro rather than the test. Nothing to fix at the call site.
#
#   bugprone-throwing-static-initialization -- Catch2's TEST_CASE registers each
#     case through a static (`dummyUser1`, ...) whose initialization the check
#     cannot prove non-throwing. 276 hits, one per TEST_CASE; the construct is
#     Catch2's, not ours.
#
#   readability-identifier-length -- 1515 hits, by far the largest single source
#     of noise, objecting to `x`, `y`, `lb`, `ub`, `id`. This is a solver: those
#     are the domain's own names and the papers the code implements use exactly
#     them. Renaming `x` to `x_var` across the DAG and the AD paths would make
#     the code read *less* like its reference. Same call, and same reasoning, as
#     the identifier-naming options below: encode the convention the project
#     actually has.
#
#   bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters -- 26 hits, of which 10 fire only
#     through modelled int<->double conversions on a solver API that is numeric
#     and positional by design. It also does not track the hazard it names:
#     SuppressParametersUsedTogether keys on the CALLEE's body rather than on
#     caller-side confusability, so ParallelSearch::solve(factory, double
#     time_limit, uint64_t seed) is silent while cbls::solve's identical pair
#     warns; and set_var(int n, int min_size, int max_size) is flagged on
#     (n, min_size) rather than on (min_size, max_size), the pair a human would
#     actually transpose. Closing the remainder means opaque handle types over
#     int32_t, which python/bindings.cpp hands to Python as plain ints -- so the
#     conversion the check models is exactly what the API boundary puts back.
#     Same call as readability-identifier-length above.
#
#     Three of the 26 were fixed anyway, on their own merit rather than for the
#     lint: make_progress deleted in favour of the SolveProgress its callers
#     already build by field name, and row_bounds / linear_row_bounds returning
#     a struct instead of a transposable pair of `double&` out-params. About
#     five genuine transposition hazards remain -- RNG::choice(n, k),
#     Model::set_var, Model::add_var_sequence, compute_partial(expr_id, var_id).
#     Guarding those is a handle-typing task, not a lint gate, and turning this
#     check on would not catch all of them anyway.
#
# Note `#` is NOT a comment inside a `>` folded scalar -- it is literal text
# that silently corrupts the check list, and a trailing `# 25` on a check line
# swallows the line after it. Every comment goes here, above the key; the
# scalar itself holds check names and nothing else. A corrupted list fails
# OPEN: checks silently stop running and a sweep goes green. Verify any edit to
# this file, and to src/io/.clang-tidy, with `clang-tidy --dump-config`.
#
# A full sweep, from a configured build directory:
#
#   .venv/bin/clang-tidy -p build --quiet $(git ls-files 'src/*.cpp' \
#       'src/io/*.cpp' 'tests/*.cpp' 'benchmarks/**/*.cpp' 'examples/*.cpp' \
#       'python/*.cpp') | grep -iE '(warning|error):' | sort -u
#
# Match case-insensitively and mind the exit status: a directory that runs no
# checks at all reports `Error: no checks enabled.` with a capital E, which a
# lower-case-only pattern misses. That is how src/io/ went unlinted for as long
# as it did. Use the pinned .venv copy -- a system clang-tidy of another
# vintage merges a different default check set.
Checks: >
  -*,
  bugprone-*,
  modernize-*,
  performance-*,
  readability-*,
  -modernize-use-trailing-return-type,
  -readability-magic-numbers,
  -bugprone-chained-comparison,
  -bugprone-throwing-static-initialization,
  -readability-identifier-length,
  -bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters

CheckOptions:
  - key: readability-function-cognitive-complexity.Threshold
    value: 25
  # IgnoreMacros stops the score counting code that came from a macro. It is
  # here for Catch2 and nothing else: clang-tidy names a TEST_CASE body after
  # the macro that generates it (`dummyFunction32`) and then charges it for
  # every SECTION's `if` and every REQUIRE's try/catch. Measured at the commit
  # that closed issue #121: 44 of that check's 71 findings were TEST_CASE
  # bodies, one of them scoring 155 off 26 assertion macros against 7 real
  # control-flow constructs. With this option all of tests/*.cpp reports zero
  # and all 27 non-test findings survive at IDENTICAL scores, so it subtracts
  # exactly the Catch2 noise and nothing else.
  #
  # It is not a way of switching the check off in tests/. A probe translation
  # unit with a deliberately convoluted TEST_CASE body and no assertion macros
  # at all still scores 47 and is still reported. That is why this is an option
  # rather than a tests/.clang-tidy exemption, which would have dropped the
  # coverage instead of the noise.
  - key: readability-function-cognitive-complexity.IgnoreMacros
    value: true
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.ClassCase
    value: CamelCase
  # Measured over include/cbls/*.h and src/*.cpp, this codebase runs 336
  # lower_case functions to 14 CamelCase (all constructors, which take the
  # class name) -- `full_evaluate`, `delta_evaluate`,
  # `weighted_violation_delta`, `augmented_objective`. camelBack would flag
  # 198 of them in src/search.cpp alone, i.e. a gate that objects to the
  # convention the project actually has. Google style permits either
  # spelling; encode the real one.
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.FunctionCase
    value: lower_case
  # The typed-variable factory API on Model is deliberately CamelCase: each
  # method names the variable type it creates (`m.Bool()`, `m.Int()`), and
  # `bool`/`int`/`float` cannot be spelled lower_case at all without
  # colliding with a keyword -- clang-tidy says so itself and declines to
  # fix them. Six methods, the only survivors of the naming sweep.
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.MethodIgnoredRegexp
    value: '^(Bool|Int|Float|List|Set|Constant)$'
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.VariableCase
    value: lower_case
  # A single ConstantCase of UPPER_CASE is a spelling used nowhere here, and
  # clang-tidy applies "constant" to every `const` local and `const`
  # parameter as well as to file-scope tables. Split by scope to match the
  # code: file-scope, static, class and constexpr constants are k-prefixed
  # CamelCase (29 measured across include/cbls and src, e.g.
  # `kMaxDeadlineStride`); `const` locals and parameters are ordinary
  # lower_case and carry no marker.
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.LocalConstantCase
    value: lower_case
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.LocalConstantPointerCase
    value: lower_case
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.ConstantParameterCase
    value: lower_case
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.ConstantPointerParameterCase
    value: lower_case
  # ConstantMemberCase is deliberately left unset. It takes precedence over
  # PrivateMemberSuffix, so setting it to lower_case would reject a `const`
  # private member spelled with this project's `_` suffix, while setting it
  # to lower_case-with-`_` would then demand the suffix on public `const`
  # members that correctly have none. Unset, const members fall through to
  # the member rules below, which is the intent.
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.GlobalConstantCase
    value: CamelCase
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.GlobalConstantPrefix
    value: k
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.StaticConstantCase
    value: CamelCase
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.StaticConstantPrefix
    value: k
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.ClassConstantCase
    value: CamelCase
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.ClassConstantPrefix
    value: k
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.ConstexprVariableCase
    value: CamelCase
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.ConstexprVariablePrefix
    value: k
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.PrivateMemberSuffix
    value: _
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.NamespaceCase
    value: lower_case

WarningsAsErrors: ''
# '.*' meant every transitively included header, so a sweep reported ~9400
# diagnostics from build/_deps (Catch2, nlohmann/json), nanobind, CPython and
# libstdc++ -- none of it code this repo can change, and enough of it to bury
# the diagnostics that are ours. Keep the broad filter and subtract what we do
# not own, so a new project header is covered by default rather than by being
# remembered.
HeaderFilterRegex: '.*'
ExcludeHeaderFilterRegex: '(build/_deps/|site-packages/|/usr/include/|/usr/lib/|/usr/bin/)'
FormatStyle: file
